# Erdős Problem #99 — Target B general coherent phase near-saturation exclusion

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Run type:** combined stability theorem  
**Target:** upstream dense-core near-saturation gate  
**Depends on:** unique-max phase stability; two-largest-tie phase stability; trimmed-grid exclusion  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN

## 1. Statement

Let a coherent three-phase train component have phase-class sizes

`M>=b>=a`

and crossing count `E`. The cyclic-order theorem gives

`E<=M(a+b)`.

Assume the component is genuinely quadratic, so

`E=Theta(M^2)`,

and that only `o(M^2)` relevant rhombus crossings lie outside the coherent component in the dense-core reduction.

### Theorem GPS1 — no coherent phase component can asymptotically saturate the cyclic-order bound

There is no sufficiently large pure-rhombic global-minimizer sequence satisfying

`E=M(a+b)-o(M^2)`.

Equivalently, along every such sequence,

`liminf [M(a+b)-E]/M^2 >0`.

The theorem covers all phase-class ratios.

## 2. Positive-density second class

Because

`E=Theta(M^2)`

and `E<=M(a+b)<=2Mb`,

the second-largest class has positive density:

`b>=tau M`

along a subsequence for some `tau>0`.

Thus complete-grid extraction from an almost-complete `M x b` pair always has macroscopic dimensions.

## 3. Case I — the largest class has a fixed gap

Suppose along a subsequence

`b/M<=1-kappa`

for some fixed `kappa>0`.

Then also `a/M<=1-kappa`. The audited unique-max stability theorem applies directly: near saturation

`E=M(a+b)-o(M^2)`

forces asymptotically complete center-to-leaf crossing pairs, ordered interval extraction produces a complete positive-density grid, and the trimmed-grid diameter theorem contradicts the global minimizer scale.

Hence this case is impossible.

## 4. Case II — the two largest classes become equal asymptotically

The only remaining possibility is

`b/M->1`.

Delete any `M-b=o(M)` trains from the largest phase class. Call the retained largest class `A_0`; now

`|A_0|=|B|=b`, `|C|=a`.

Deleting `o(M)` trains removes at most

`(M-b)(a+b)=o(M^2)`

phase crossings. Therefore the retained component has

`E_0 >= E-o(M^2)`.

The extremal bound changes by exactly

`M(a+b)-b(a+b)=(M-b)(a+b)=o(M^2)`.

Hence

`E_0=b(a+b)-o(M^2)`.

Since `b~M`, the retained component is still quadratic and still carries all but `o(b^2)` of the relevant coherent-core crossings. The removed `o(M)` train vectors contribute only `o(b)` to any trimmed-grid diagonal and are harmless in the geometric error term.

The audited two-largest-tie theorem now applies to the retained class sizes

`b,b,a`

and gives a contradiction.

Thus this case is also impossible.

## 5. Exhaustion

Every sequence has a subsequence on which either

1. `b/M<=1-kappa` for some fixed `kappa>0`, or
2. `b/M->1`.

Sections 3 and 4 exclude both possibilities. This proves GPS1.

## 6. Uniform-gap corollary

Fix any positive-density threshold `tau>0` and the standard dense-core coherence/error hypotheses. If no asymptotic positive gap existed, there would be a sequence of admissible components with

`b/M>=tau`

and

`[M(a+b)-E]/M^2 ->0`,

contradicting GPS1.

Therefore the coherent phase endpoint has a genuine stability gap: for each fixed dense-core regime there is some

`delta_phase>0`

such that all sufficiently large admissible components obey

`E <= M(a+b)-delta_phase M^2`.

No explicit numerical value of `delta_phase` is claimed; obtaining one would require quantitative coherence and pruning moduli.

## 7. What remains upstream

The phase equality/stability problem is now closed conditionally on reaching one coherent component near its local cyclic-order bound.

The remaining Target B gate is a **global deficit-transfer theorem**:

> Starting from Theorem 50.1, the shell/phase-energy bounds, and the fact that the retained dense core contains all but arbitrarily small `O(D^2)` crossing mass, show that the sum of local phase deficits
+>
+> `sum_H [M_H(T_H-M_H)-E(H)]`
+>
+> cannot stay bounded below by a positive multiple of `D^2` in a hypothetical global minimizer.

GPS1 says that every quadratic coherent component actually contributes such a positive-density local deficit. A successful summation of those deficits into the existing global crossing/shell inequalities would now give the desired contradiction.
